er**:\n   \\[ A = 9\\pi + 15\\pi = 24\\pi \\]\n\n5. **Approximate the surface area**:\n   \\[ A \\approx 24 \\times 3.14159 = 75.39822 \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the cone is approximately \\( 75.398 \\) square units.\n\nIf you have specific dimensions for a cone, you can follow these steps to find the surface area. If you need further assistance or have another example, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. It really helped me understand how to calculate the surface area of a cone.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. Understanding how to calculate the surface area of a cone is useful in many real-world applications, such as in geometry, physics, and engineering. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you explain how to calculate the volume of a pyramid?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The volume of a pyramid can be calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( V \\) is the volume,\n- \\(\\text{Base Area}\\) is the area of the base of the pyramid,\n- \\(\\text{Height}\\) is the perpendicular distance from the base to the apex of the pyramid.\n\nLet's break down the steps to calculate the volume of a pyramid:\n\n1. **Identify the base and height**: You need to know the area of the base and the height of the pyramid.\n2. **Calculate the base area**: Use the appropriate formula for the area of the base (e.g., for a square base, use \\( \\text{Base Area} = s^2 \\) where \\( s \\) is the side length; for a triangular base, use the formula for the area of a triangle).\n3. **Multiply the base area by the height**: Multiply the base area by the height.\n4. **Multiply by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)**: Finally, multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nLet's go through an example to illustrate this process.\n\n### Example:\nSup